---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../components/Crumbs.astro';
import { SITE, url } from '../lib/site';
import ecosystem from '../data/ecosystem.json';
---

<Base title="About Charitology" description="What Charitology is, how it works, how it stays honest, and the open instruments it builds on.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Crumbs items={[{ label: 'About' }]} />
      <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
      <h1>An open science of doing the most good</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Charitology studies charity as a system: where generosity flows, why it flows there, and how to route it from the
        symptoms of the world’s problems to their sources.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap section-tight">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What it is</h2>
      <p>
        Charitology is a field, not a charity. It doesn’t run programs on the ground, rate organizations or ask for money. It
        publishes a <a href={url('/diagnosis/')}>diagnosis</a> of the charity system, a <a href={url('/method/')}>method</a>
        anyone can use, <a href={url('/open-problems/')}>open problems</a> for researchers, free <a href={url('/tools/')}>tools</a
        > for givers and organizations, and a growing <a href={url('/record/')}>Record</a> of what is known and what isn’t.
      </p>

      <h2>Why now</h2>
      <p>
        In October 2026 the obvious name for the science of doing the most good was available to register for about ten
        dollars. Read the founding manifesto, <a href={url('/manifesto/')}>“Ten Dollars”</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How it stays honest</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No judgment of the good being done.</strong> The subject is the system, not the people in it.</li>
        <li><strong>Every claim is sourced and labeled</strong> established, documented, debated or hypothesis.</li>
        <li><strong>No ratings of named charities</strong> until a rating method has survived public red-team review.</li>
        <li><strong>Corrections and challenges are public.</strong> Every page links to both.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Edition 0 was drafted with AI assistance</strong> and is being reviewed in public. Pages say whether a second
          contributor has reviewed them yet.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The open instruments it builds on</h2>
      <p>
        Charitology links independent open projects that already measure parts of the system. Each keeps its own method and
        license.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          ecosystem.instruments.map((i) => (
            <li>
              <a href={i.url}>{i.name}</a>: {i.summary}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        No cookies, analytics, ads or accounts. The tools run in your browser, and anything you save stays in your browser’s
        storage. The site is a set of static files hosted on GitHub Pages.
      </p>

      <h2>Licenses</h2>
      <p>
        Code is MIT-licensed. Content and data are <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>: use
        them for anything, with credit to “Charitology (charitology.org)”. Sources quoted or cited keep their own rights.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Everything happens in the open on <a href={SITE.repo}>GitHub</a>: issues for corrections and ideas, pull requests for
        changes, discussions for everything else.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
